<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="superpathway of proto-and siroheme biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Description>&lt;b&gt;General background&lt;/b&gt;: Heme (protoheme, heme &lt;i&gt;b&lt;/i&gt;) is an iron-containing prosthetic group found in many essential proteins including cytochromes and heme-containing globins. It is a porphyrin member of the cyclic tetrapyrroles and biosynthesized as protoheme. Siroheme is an iron-containing isobacteriochlorin, and constitutes a modified tetrapyrrole similar in structure to both heme and chlorophyll. &lt;b&gt;About the pathway&lt;/b&gt;: Siroheme biosynthesis starts by the same route as other tetrapyrroles, i.e. |FRAME:GLT L-glutamate| and branches out on the intermediate |FRAME: UROPORPHYRINOGEN-III uroporphyrinogen-III|. Methylation of |FRAME: UROPORPHYRINOGEN-III uroporphyrinogen-III| directs it toward siroheme biosynthesis, a heme-like prosthetic group that is found in only two but indispensable plant enzymes, sulfite and nitrite reductases |CITS: [9006913]|. On the other side decarboxylation of |FRAME: UROPORPHYRINOGEN-III uroporphyrinogen-III| results in |FRAME: HEME-BIOSYNTHESIS-II heme &lt;i&gt;b&lt;/i&gt; biosynthesis I (aerobic)| |CITS: [17416636]|. A second branch point at |FRAME: PROTOPORPHYRIN_IX protoporphyrin IX| leads to heme biosynthesis. Two enzymes at that branchpoint, i.e. ferrochelatase |CITS: [17416636]| and Mg-chelatase |CITS: [Walker97]| decide whether protoporphyrin IX is channeled towards Mg-containing chlorophyllides or Fe-containing hemes. Two more enzymes involved in the heme biosynthesis have been characterized in &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt; so far. Coproporphyrinogen III oxidase, which forms protoporphyrinogen IX by oxidative decarboxylation |CITS: [Santana02]| and protoporphyrinogen oxidase generating protoporphyrin IX the aforementioned branchpoint compound of the pathway |CITS: [8982084]|. For siroheme synthesis, methylation occurs at positions 2 and 7, forming dihydrosirohydrochlorin (also known as precorrin-2) |CITS: [8953249]| |CITS: [9006913]|. The second step of the three step pathway to siroheme, the oxidation of precorrin-2 to sirohydrochlorin is catalyzed by precorrin-2 dehydrogenase, an enzyme that has not yet been characterized in plants. The final step, the chalation of ferrous iron to sirohydrochlorin forming siroheme is catalyzed by sirohydrochlorin ferrochelatase possessing an integral Fe-S center which has been discussed as indication to the close association with the metabolism of sulfur and iron |CITS: [15545265]|.</Description>
