    <DataNode elementId="AT2G30390-MONOMER" textLabel="ferrochelatase II" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="O04921" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="300" centerY="555" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">The cDNA encoding ferrochelatase II (FC2), one out of two ferrochelatases found in the genome of &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt; has been isolated and partially characterized. FC2 catalyzes the last step of the heme biosynthesis, i.e. the insertion of ferrous iron into protoporphyrin IX to form protoheme. The molecular function could be confirmed by functional complementation of a yeast mutant lacking ferrochelatase activity |CITS: [9753778]|. FC2 has been demonstrated to be located in chloroplasts |CITS: [11602264]| more specifically in the thylakoid, the stroma and the envelope |CITS: [9753778]|. Ferrochelatase 2 is expressed in aerial and green tissues of the plant, but not in roots |CITS: [12374307]|. The difference in expression pattern and localization between the two ferrochelatases has been interpreted as a division of the corresponding function. FC2 has been shown to be correlated with the entry enzyme of the |FRAME:PWY-5188|, i.e. glutamyl-tRNA reductase (HEMA1) and is involved under normal conditions in heme biosynthesis in photosynthetic tissues. However, under oxidative stress, FC2 together with HEMA1 is down-regulated and their function is partly adopted by the other pair of ferrochelatase and glutamyl-tRNA reductase FC1 and HEMA 2 |CITS: [17416636]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT2G30390-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Araport" value="AT2G30390.2" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Uniprot" value="O04921" />
